    "available_at": "2026-11-25T16:18:45.154314+00:00",
    "body": "View of ValOre's Pedra Branca property in Brazil. Credit: ValOre Metals.\nPosted By: FREDERIC TOMESCO November 25, 2026\nCanadian developer ValOre Metals (TSXV: VO; US-OTC: KVLQF) agreed to buy the Água Azul gold project in Brazil for $135 million (C$193 million) — more than 20 times the company’s market value before trading was halted Tuesday.\nVancouver-based ValOre plans to buy Bemisa Água Azul Mineração and an associated landholding entity from the Opportunity Holding investment fund, according to a statement Tuesday. The agreement will require a $90 million payment at closing, followed by $20 million after one year and $25 million after two years, subject to adjustments.\nValOre, which still needs to secure acquisition funding, expects the deal to close by Jan. 15, subject to TSX Venture Exchange acceptance. “Advanced” financing discussions covering the purchase, working capital and initial development spending are ongoing, the company said.\nUnder the agreement, ValOre has a one-time option within four months of closing to settle all $45 million of deferred payments for $30 million, subject to United States inflation adjustments. Exercising it would cut the nominal purchase price to $120 million.\nOperating asset\nLocated about 1,900 km northwest of Sao Paulo, Água Azul would give ValOre a second Brazilian property, as well as a first operating asset. The company is already developing the Pedra Branca platinum group elements project in Ceará state, about 200 km from Fortaleza. Água Azul includes a 206-sq.-km land package that hosts the Abelhas, Juliano and Campos Altos deposit areas along a 15-km structural trend. Its Abelhas open-pit gold mine feeds a plant processing about 120,000 tonnes of material a year.\nA July 28 preliminary economic assessment evaluates increasing the plant’s processing capacity to 2.85 million tonnes a year, following a three-year development period.\nThe study estimates $297 million in initial capital, including plant expansion, pre-stripping and contingency. At a 5% discount rate and a long-term gold price of $3,609 per oz., it calculates an after-tax net present value of $927.6 million and a 48% internal rate of return.\nMine schedule\nÁgua Azul’s mine schedule spans 13 years, including about a decade at the expanded capacity. Estimated all-in sustaining costs are $1,540 per ounce. The assessment is preliminary and includes inferred resources, which are too speculative to support mineral reserves.\nThe property holds 22.8 million indicated tonnes grading 1.27 grams per tonne gold containing 931,920 oz. gold, according to a July resource. Inferred resources were estimated at 26.4 million tonnes grading 1.29 grams gold for contained metal of 1.1 million oz. gold.\nThe acquisition comes as ValOre advances processing research at Pedra Branca. On Nov. 10, it announced a five-year cooperation agreement with Germany’s federal geoscience institute and mineral resources agency through the government-funded BioPlat research project.\nShares of ValOre were halted Tuesday because the deal constitutes a fundamental acquisition under TSX Venture Exchange rules. They closed at 3.5¢ on Monday, valuing the company at C$8.9 million ($6.2 million).\nValOre expects the trading halt to last until closing.\nPrint\nTAG: Brazil gold exploration gold mines gold mining gold production gold projects M&A mining M&A\nCOMMODITY: Gold Platinum Group Metals\nREGION: Canada South America\nCOMPANY: ValOre Metals\nRepublish this article",
